module Core

# ============================================================================
# Correctly rounded natural exponential of B16
# ============================================================================
# Ported from CORE-MATH, src/binary16/exp/expf16.c (https://core-math.gitlabpages.inria.fr/).
# Copyright (c) 2025 Maxence Ponsardin and Paul Zimmermann. Distributed under the MIT license: see LICENSE-CORE-MATH next to this file.
# RazorForge has no dynamic rounding modes, so only the round-to-nearest path of the original is kept (its
# errno and exception-flag handling are dropped). Arithmetic uses the unchecked operators `+! -! *! /!`,
# which are raw IEEE 754 on floats.

### Returns e raised to this value, correctly rounded: the exact result rounded to the nearest `B16` (ties to even),
### identical on every platform. Overflows to +infinity, underflows to 0, and a NaN input gives
### a NaN.
### :returns: The correctly rounded exp.
routine B16.exp_unchecked() -> B16
  # x = x1 + x2, where x1 keeps the upper 11 bits of the encoding and x2 the sign, the exponent and the
  # lower 5 bits: exp(x) = exp(x1) * exp(x2), both tabulated as B32 approximations
  var u = LLVM::zero_extend[U16, U64](me.to_bits())
  var i1 = u >>> 5
  var i2 = ((u >>> 10) << 5) | (u & 0x1F_u64)
  # a NaN input: both table entries may be NaN patterns, so the NaN is picked explicitly instead of
  # relying on the platform's NaN propagation
  if me.is_nan()
    return b16_exp_pick_nan(first: B16_EXP_T1[i1], second: B16_EXP_T2[i2])
  return B16(from: B32.from_bits(bits: B16_EXP_T1[i1]) *! B32.from_bits(bits: B16_EXP_T2[i2]))

### The result for a NaN input: the B32 table entry `first` when it is a NaN, else `second` (then a NaN
### too), quieted and narrowed to B16 by truncating its payload, as the x86 reference does. Here that is
### `T1[i1]`, else `T2[i2]`.
### :param first: The B32 bit pattern tried first.
### :param second: The B32 bit pattern used when `first` is not a NaN.
### :returns: The quiet B16 NaN.
secret routine b16_exp_pick_nan(first: U32, second: U32) -> B16
  var n = second
  if (first & 0x7FFFFFFF_u32) > 0x7F800000_u32
    n = first
  n = n | 0x00400000_u32
  var h = ((n >>> 16) & 0x8000_u32) | 0x7C00_u32 | ((n >>> 13) & 0x3FF_u32)
  return B16.from_bits(bits: LLVM::int_truncate[U32, U16](h))

### B32 bit patterns of exp(x1), where the B16 encoding of x1 is `i1 * 2^5` (capped to the
